## The source order

When two sources disagree, the higher one wins. A kind of fact owned by a live system is
always read there; the vault holds a dated copy at most.

| Kind of fact | Owned by | The vault holds |
|---|---|---|
| Money: balances, invoices, payments | ~~accounting ([TOOL OR none]) | Rules and pointers only, never figures |
| Customers and contacts | ~~CRM ([TOOL OR none]) | Person and company pages, checked monthly |
| Dates and meetings | ~~calendar ([TOOL OR none]) | Nothing; dates are read live |
| Documents | ~~file storage ([TOOL]) | The originals, in place |
| How we work, what we decided | the vault | Knowledge and decision pages |

Then, among vault pages: verified beats unreviewed; newer verified beats older; a decision
beats a page it changed.

## The ten rules, as [COMPANY] applies them

1. Newest verified wins. A page past its review date is never the source for a number that leaves.
2. Conflicts follow the source order above; say which won and log it.
3. Say "I don't know", and offer the way to find it.
4. Never turn a guess into company knowledge. Every page has a source.
5. Supersede, never overwrite a fact.
6. Deletion is a human act.
7. A new knowledge page needs two independent sources, or one decision it changes.
8. Show the files to be changed, then apply once.
9. Sources are never edited.
10. Private stays private. Confidential pages go to [OWNER] only. Nothing crosses to a public folder except through `/release`.

